WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.302 --> 00:00:05.351
- [Narrator] This is Olivia,
an East African Crowned Crane.

00:00:05.351 --> 00:00:06.684
She is stunning.

00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:14.001
She stands about four feet tall

00:00:14.001 --> 00:00:17.101
with a wingspan of six and a half feet.

00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:18.709
Her legs are long and slender,

00:00:18.709 --> 00:00:22.052
perfect for wading through grassy areas.

00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:24.488
Mostly gray, the wings
are predominantly white

00:00:24.488 --> 00:00:25.903
with a range of colors,

00:00:25.903 --> 00:00:29.077
while her head is full of golden feathers.

00:00:29.077 --> 00:00:31.104
East African Crowned Cranes are well-known

00:00:31.104 --> 00:00:33.291
for their elaborate dancing.

00:00:33.291 --> 00:00:34.782
These cranes are omnivores,

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:37.173
eating everything from plants to insects,

00:00:37.173 --> 00:00:40.134
snakes, small fish, and more.

00:00:40.134 --> 00:00:42.580
Native to eastern sub-Saharan Africa,

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:44.159
and down to South Africa,

00:00:44.159 --> 00:00:47.935
they prefer living in
grassland areas near the water.

00:00:47.935 --> 00:00:50.356
The Crowned Crane is the
national bird of Uganda,

00:00:50.356 --> 00:00:52.273
featured on its flag.

00:00:52.273 --> 00:00:55.767
But even with such iconic
status, we're losing them.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:56.813
In less than 10 years,

00:00:56.813 --> 00:01:00.707
their population has
decreased by over 50%.

00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:02.499
Habitat loss and illegal removal

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:06.611
of eggs and birds from the
wild are its biggest threats.

00:01:06.611 --> 00:01:09.912
This is the East African Crowned Crane.

00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:13.745
(gentle, inspirational music)

